on/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168140" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](images/1b/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} {:answer/id #uuid "28248254-c521-555f-a8eb-3698fd411628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336409} {:answer/id #uuid "54358dc3-968d-51c2-b7a6-0a9e65676557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336809} {:answer/id #uuid "559f9ad0-2178-5631-a30c-df72edf7e3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336818} {:answer/id #uuid "55dfee5e-aee0-54ba-a2ef-8bb2f5c930f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17be251d-7119-505a-822a-7b4f4a540bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336254} :answer-field/id #uuid "acf0a001-ed03-592c-9465-79a8c55ab0a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcd8034-22f3-5298-b8d3-b69cdcbc84fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72185" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region inside the circumference ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/09/0960063edcce8ccd55dc2f77147c4a4982645c180e8b998d7e520c65131ea045.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b81e3f-060b-5845-a303-b88224da1581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336078} {:answer/id #uuid "46f070d3-ed05-5034-af82-a9adf57c60b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336690} {:answer/id #uuid "4deab650-a626-55f2-b9af-f7b4ccb3ee34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336755} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1230df-1cda-5e95-8f8f-00f39e16c196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336882} {:answer/id #uuid "cf98decd-9264-56e3-850f-4cfdc80409db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f070d3-ed05-5034-af82-a9adf57c60b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336690} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cff232-9d15-5600-89b1-e7cd0fac0002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b03842bf-e94f-5526-903d-de5a2a3bccca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/28/28290c5530efd27a979f4284481ca7e0384342d7d426dde0ab36894a0d77a166.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/30/3007cffb725faee44d2a06ad077eec630791b1a94c373a323f62d0d721c87207.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/38/383f88668435556d3a856cf852caf548b1f1bf3a90d50f9d85d7968188984804.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}]}
